{"judgments": [
  {"clause_id": "c001",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The preamble assigns CSi Agency Services the defined short name \"CS\" while the rest of the instrument uses \"CSi\", and the parenthetical for Alpha Partners is malformed (\"Alpha Partners, Ltd, Alpha\")). These are mechanical slips in the definitional apparatus, though the intended referents are obvious from context.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.14},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.06}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c002",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The recital defines \"the reinsurance relationship\" by an enumerated list of agreements and addenda prefaced by \"including, but not limited to,\" leaving the reach of the catch-all over other addenda uncertain. It also names State and County Mutual as a party to that relationship though it is not a party to this MOU.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22},
     {"issue": "The recital's defined \"reinsurance relationship\" is never used in the operative sections, which instead recite the individual agreements (Sections I and III), so the two descriptions of scope must be harmonised. No square contradiction appears.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c003",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.05},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.06}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c004",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The recital says the business solution \"involves the payment, going forward, beginning May 4, 2009, of losses and all fees and expenses and costs associated in any way with\" the reinsured policies without saying who must pay, and \"associated in any way\" has no discernible boundary. The payor and the outer edge of the covered costs are both left to construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.33},
     {"issue": "The recital's picture of a global forward-looking payment solution sits against Section III's final sentence, which preserves \"any obligations of U.S. Auto, Gamma, or any other defendant,\" so the recital may be read to allocate to Santa Fe what the operative text leaves open. A court could be asked whether the recital colours Sections I and III.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c005",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The obligation is to \"enter into\" an LPT whose terms are not set out here, so whether this is a binding transfer of liability or only an agreement to agree turns on words outside the clause. \"All past and future losses ... whether or not known\" is broad but is not itself indefinite.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
     {"issue": "Section IX describes the same instrument as a \"mutually agreeable reinsurance agreement/LPT\" whose non-completion merely lets Lincoln refile, which pulls against the mandatory \"shall enter into\" here and against Section V's stipulation that the MOU is enforceable. Reconciling the command with the escape hatch is a genuine construction problem.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.45}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c006",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The carve-out for \"payments completed prior to May 4, 2009\" does not say whose payments, and the following sentence defines completion only by reference to checks issued by Santa Fe. The exclusion's application to payments made by Lincoln General is therefore uncertain.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34},
     {"issue": "The exclusion must be squared with the ZBA reimbursement sentence of Section I, which makes Santa Fe reimburse \"all payments out of the ZBA from May 4, 2009,\" leaving the treatment of checks issued before but cleared after that date contestable. The two sentences use different payment concepts.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.30}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c007",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The definition says \"a payment is considered to have been 'completed' when the check or payment has been dated and issued by Santa Fe,\" so a payment made by anyone other than Santa Fe has no defined completion date. Whether \"dated and issued\" requires both, and on which date, is also open.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.36},
     {"issue": "Because Section I elsewhere speaks of payments out of Lincoln General's ZBA, the Santa Fe-only definition cannot be applied to those payments without strain. The definition and the ZBA reimbursement sentence assume different payors.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c008",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The permission runs \"until the LPT is executed, but no later than June 30, 2009,\" which does not say what happens to claim payments if the LPT is not executed by that date. The gap is real but small.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.24},
     {"issue": "Section IX contemplates that the LPT may never be executed, yet this clause cuts off ZBA use on June 30, 2009 regardless, leaving claims funding unaddressed in that interval. The two provisions assume different futures.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.26}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c009",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"The date of execution\" is unqualified and could refer to execution of the LPT (the subject of the preceding sentence) or of the MOU itself. The date on which the ZBA must go dark turns on which is meant.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.36},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.12}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c010",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The reimbursement window runs \"from May 4, 2009 until the date of execution,\" repeating the unqualified phrase \"the date of execution\" (LPT or MOU), so the end of Santa Fe's reimbursement obligation is uncertain. No timing or mechanism for reimbursement is stated either.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34},
     {"issue": "The obligation to reimburse \"all payments out of the ZBA from May 4, 2009\" overlaps with the Section I carve-out for payments \"completed\" before May 4, 2009, and the two use inconsistent tests for when a payment counts. A court could be asked to reconcile them.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.28}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c011",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.10},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.10}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c012",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Other investments acceptable to Lincoln General\" and \"an A rated reinsurer\" are both undefined — the first gives unbounded discretion with no reasonableness qualifier, the second does not identify the rating agency or the timing of the rating. Either could be read differently by the parties.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.38},
     {"issue": "Elsewhere in Section II Lincoln General's approvals are expressly qualified as \"not to be unreasonably withheld\" (actuary selection and trust release), while the acceptability of investments and the withdrawal consent here carry no such qualifier. The differing treatment invites an argument about implied good faith.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c013",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "The clause requires that Lincoln General \"be a named party\" to and \"participate in the drafting and wording of\" the reinsurance agreement without stating any standard or consequence if participation is refused. \"Directly obligated\" for amounts owed by the reinsurer to Santa Fe is a cut-through of uncertain scope.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.11}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c014",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Default on its obligations\" is not defined and \"the amount of future losses forecast\" gives no forecast period, so the collateral trigger and quantum are both left to argument. The $3 million cap is clear, but what sits beneath it is not.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.31},
     {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.12}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c015",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"Those funds may be released\" does not say whether the whole trust or only the excess over estimated remaining liability is released, nor who may compel a release. Read literally the entire $3 million could leave the trust the moment its value exceeds the actuarial estimate.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.42},
     {"issue": "A release of the whole fund would defeat the Section II requirement that Santa Fe \"provide security in the amount of $3 million\" as security for the LPT, so the release mechanic and the security obligation pull against each other. Harmonising them requires reading in an excess-only limit.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.36}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c016",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"This release shall be performed annually\" makes mandatory what the preceding sentence made permissive (\"may be released\"), and does not say from which date the year runs or who performs it. The mandatory/permissive mismatch is the point of contest.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.34},
     {"issue": "The sentence cannot be squared with the immediately preceding release sentence of Section II, which says funds \"may be released\" only if the actuarial test is met, since an annual mandatory release would operate regardless. The two sentences of the same paragraph conflict.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
   ]},
  {"clause_id": "c017",
   "issues": [
     {"issue": "\"All activities in connection with the investigation, settlement, contesting, or handling of claims\" is broad but the funding duty has no cap, term, or mechanism. The breadth is deliberate rather than ambiguous.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.22},
     {"issue": "Santa Fe's open-ended funding duty here overlaps with its LPT liability under Section I and its ZBA reimbursement duty, raising a question whether the obligations are cumulative or the same money twice. Nothing expressly coordinates them.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
   ]}
]}

